   457|                        </tr>
   458|                        <tr>
   459|                            <td>Minor procedure (day surgery)</td>
   460|                            <td>&#x00A5;5,000-&#x00A5;20,000</td>
   461|                            <td>$700-$2,800</td>
   462|                        </tr>
   463|                        <tr>
   464|                            <td>Intermediate surgery (1-3 day stay)</td>
   465|                            <td>&#x00A5;20,000-&#x00A5;50,000</td>
   466|                            <td>$2,800-$7,000</td>
   467|                        </tr>
   468|                        <tr>
   469|                            <td>Major surgery (extended stay)</td>
   470|                            <td>&#x00A5;50,000-&#x00A5;100,000</td>
   471|                            <td>$7,000-$14,000</td>
   472|                        </tr>
   473|                        <tr>
   474|                            <td>Complex/Cardiac/Transplant</td>
   475|                            <td>&#x00A5;100,000-&#x00A5;200,000+</td>
   476|                            <td>$14,000-$28,000+</td>
   477|                        </tr>
   478|                    </tbody>
   479|                </table>
   480|            </section>
   481|
   482|            <!-- Section 3: Payment Methods -->
   483|            <section id="section3">
   484|                <h2>3. Payment Methods for International Patients</h2>
   485|
   486|                <p>China's payment infrastructure differs significantly from Western countries, and understanding your options for transferring funds to Chinese hospitals is essential for smooth financial settlement. Each payment method has distinct advantages, limitations, and processing timelines that affect when and how you can settle your medical bills.</p>
   487|
   488|                <h3>Chinese Bank Transfer</h3>
   489|                <p>A domestic Chinese bank transfer (&#x56FD;&#x5185;&#x94F6;&#x884C;&#x8F6C;&#x8D26;) is the most straightforward method for larger hospital payments. However, this requires you to have or open a Chinese bank account, which involves identity verification (需要身份证件) and takes several business days for a foreign national.</p>
   490|
   491|                <p>The process typically involves visiting a bank branch in person with your passport and proof of address. Major banks offering services to foreigners include ICBC, China Construction Bank, Bank of China, and HSBC China. Once you have an account, transfers within China are instant or same-day and carry minimal fees (typically &#x00A5;0-&#x00A5;50 per transfer regardless of amount).</p>
   492|
   493|                <div class="info-box warning">
   494|                    <h4>&#x26A0; Bank Account Opening Requirements</h4>
   495|                    <p>Chinese banks require visitors to provide their passport, a Chinese phone number (required for SMS verification), and often proof of local address (hotel booking or rental agreement). Some hospitals can assist with this process through their international department. Budget 3-5 business days for account setup if you need to open an account specifically for medical payments.</p>
   496|                </div>
   497|
   498|                <h3>Alipay and WeChat Pay</h3>
   499|                <p>Alipay (&#x652F;&#x4ED8;&#x5B9D;) and WeChat Pay (&#x5FAE;&#x4FE1;&#x652F;&#x4ED8;) dominate Chinese digital payments, but their functionality for foreign visitors has historically been limited. As of recent updates, both platforms do offer tourist versions that link to foreign credit cards, though with restrictions compared to the full Chinese citizen versions.</p>
